From cfaad5f1ca3a4e2739a1a9ac13df7571522c1e13 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Niri-RDP rebase <niri-rdp@localhost>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 14:16:52 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 12/12] image-copy-capture: include the drag-and-drop icon in
 cursor captures

render_pointer() draws the drag-and-drop icon at its offset from the
pointer together with the cursor, so screencopy with overlay_cursor and
screen output show it. Cursor capture sessions only rendered the cursor
image, so a client that draws the cursor itself from a cursor session and
captures the output without it (the Niri-RDP bridge) lost drag icons.

While a drag is active, the cursor capture now covers the union of the
cursor image and the icon's surface tree (plus the pointer pixel, so the
hotspot stays inside the buffer when the cursor is hidden). The buffer
size and hotspot follow that area, and the icon is rendered below the
cursor like in render_pointer(). Without a drag nothing changes. Icon
appearance, movement and removal go through the existing size-change
(buffer_constraints) and damage paths.

This keeps the drag icon that the former niri-rdp-cursor-v1 protocol
included in its cursor shape.
